## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
<p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Historical Building.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write emails, letters, or texts describing past events. Interactive: Write a class project proposal with guidance.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a letter describing a visit to a local artist (e.g., "Yesterday, we visited an artist who makes molas"). Can write brief messages about past art or craft events (e.g., "Last week, our class made baskets"). Can draft a proposal for a project on local art with support (e.g., "We want to study Guna Yala art").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of famous historical buildings in Panama (e.g., Casco Antiguo, Panama Canal Administration Building) and also local crafts (e.g., molas, tagua carvings, baskets). Students orally identify the images they recognize. The teacher asks, "What kind of art or craft can you find in these places?" or "Who creates these beautiful things?" to activate prior knowledge of vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher projects a short descriptive text about a historical building that houses an art gallery, focusing on how past events and artistic achievements are described. For example: "This old building, originally built in 1900, *has recently displayed* an exhibition of local crafts. Last year, a famous *artist* *painted* a large *mural* on the north wall. The building is a two-story structure, with a rectangular design and white stone facades." The teacher highlights words like "displayed," "painted," "artist," "mural," and "building." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like "sculpture" (/skʌlp.tʃər/) and "design" (/dɪ'zaɪn/), emphasizing syllable stress, and the inflectional endings in past tense verbs like "painted" and "displayed."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of text did we just read? Is it a story or a description?" (Expected answer: "A description.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the text talk about something happening now or in the past?" (Expected answer: "Both, present and past.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short email from a student to their art teacher proposing a class project on "Guna Yala art." The email includes descriptions of past visits and achievements, and a proposal with initial steps. Genre: Informal email. Register: Semi-formal. Format: Digital text on screen or handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Example Email Snippet:**</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Subject: Idea for a Class Art Project</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Dear Ms. Ana,</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">I hope you are doing well. Last week, our class *visited* the local *gallery* and saw an amazing *exhibition* of Panamanian *crafts*. It was very *inspiring*! I *have often thought* about the beautiful *molas* we saw. This *artist* *has created* truly *unique* pieces. I want to propose a class project on Guna Yala art. We could start by drawing geometric shapes, then cut out the fabric. What do you think?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Sincerely,</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Juan</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing achievements of artists: "This artist has created beautiful sculptures." Describing how to create something: "First, you prepare the materials and soak the fibers." Propose and develop steps for art projects in class while engaging in feedback exchanges.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the email individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of Juan's email?" (Students raise hands to answer: "To propose an art project.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Juan visited the gallery two months ago." (False) "Juan's project idea is about Guna Yala art." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the bolded words in the email (e.g., gallery, exhibition, artist, molas, unique) to their definitions provided on the board or handout.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Present perfect, Present and past simple, Imperatives. Vocabulary: artist, craft, basket, sculpture, gallery, studio, exhibition, canvas, building, mural, mola, tagua carving, create, paint, sculpt, design, carve, display, creative, artistic, handmade, colorful, abstract, bold, vibrant, minimalist, timeless, unique, detailed. Pragmatic: Describing creations, achievements, and how to create something.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences using the correct form of verbs (past simple, present perfect) and appropriate vocabulary from a word bank.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "Last year, the ______ (artist/mural) _______ (paint/carve) a beautiful ______ (sculpture/mural) on the old ______ (canvas/building) wall." (Expected: artist, painted, mural, building)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "This local ______ (craft/gallery) ______ (create/has created) many ______ (unique/bold) ______ (baskets/exhibitions) over the years." (Expected: artist, has created, unique, baskets)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "To make a _______ (mola/sculpture), first, you _______ (prepare/design) the fabric." (Expected: mola, prepare)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Descriptive Sentence Writing:</b> Students work individually to write 3-4 descriptive sentences about a historical building or a piece of art shown by the teacher (e.g., a photo of a Panamanian *mola* or a *tagua carving*). They must use at least one present simple, one past simple, and one present perfect sentence. They also need to incorporate at least three vocabulary words (e.g., "colorful," "handmade," "display").</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompt: "Describe this mola and where you might see it." (Student might write: "This mola is very *colorful* and *handmade*. An *artist* *has designed* these geometric shapes. Last year, I saw a similar *mola* in an *exhibition*.")</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Peer Review of Descriptions:</b> Students exchange their descriptive sentences from activity 2 with a partner. They read each other's sentences, checking for correct use of grammar (past simple, present perfect, imperatives), vocabulary, and clarity. They give constructive feedback using simple phrases like "I like your description of the colors," or "Maybe you can add a verb here."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Project Proposal Drafting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs or small groups (3 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario:</b> Their class wants to propose an "Arts and Crafts Week" project to the school principal. They need to draft a short proposal (a letter or an email, about 80-100 words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instructions:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The proposal must include:
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A brief description of a historical building or local art/craft they have recently studied or visited (using past simple or present perfect, e.g., "Last month, we visited an old *building* that *has displayed* many *timeless* *sculptures*.").</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A suggestion for an art project related to local *crafts* (e.g., *mola* making, *basket* weaving, *tagua carving*).</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two steps explaining *how to create something* using imperatives (e.g., "First, *prepare* the materials. Then, *design* your pattern.").</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least five target vocabulary words (e.g., *artist, craft, gallery, exhibition, creative, unique*).</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support with vocabulary and grammar, and encouraging collaboration.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, collaborative interaction within the group, and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to use the pragmatic functions of describing creations, achievements, and giving instructions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the production stage and a simple rubric for evaluating the written project proposal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups submit their written project proposal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric focused on writing):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., artist, mola, sculpture, building, unique, colorful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct application of present perfect and past simple to describe events/achievements, and imperatives for instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Proposal is clear, easy to understand, and ideas are logically organized.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Includes all required elements (description of building/art, project suggestion, steps for creation).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or grammar point you used in your writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel describing a past art event in English (1=not confident, 5=very confident)?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing the project proposal, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned today that you can use to describe art or historical buildings in the future?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The practice activities (sentence completion and descriptive writing) worked well to build confidence before the main writing task. Students particularly enjoyed writing about historical buildings and local crafts."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Some groups struggled with consistently applying the present perfect versus past simple. I need to provide an additional mini-lesson or a quick reference chart for this grammar point in the next lesson's warm-up if needed, focusing on their specific errors. The pronunciation practice of inflectional endings was helpful during modeling, but less explicitly applied during the writing task; perhaps I can incorporate it when students read their proposals aloud for feedback in Lesson 5."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"For the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will summarize information from these proposals and present their ideas, which will be a good way to reinforce both spoken and written elements of describing art and projects. The project proposals drafted today will serve as direct input for the mediation task."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>